+++ b/docs/perf/ghost-network.md
@@ -0,0 +1,132 @@
+# Ghost preview network traffic
+
+These are estimates worked out on paper, not measurements. Roblox doesn't publish its exact wire
+format, so the per-message sizes below are rough. They're good enough to compare the two designs,
+but confirm them in a multi-client Studio test or a live server using the Developer Console (F9)
+→ Network, or the Shift+F3 network stats.
+
+## What changed
+
+| | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| Client sends | `MoveGhost(name, Vector3)` every `RenderStepped`, even when the cursor is still or placing is off | `GhostUpdate(blockIndex, cell)` only when the cell, block type or visibility changes, and no more than 20 per second |
+| Server | Moves a replicated ghost `Part` per player on every message | Keeps the latest state per player. Up to 20 times a second, it sends everything that changed since the last send in one `FireAllClients` (a `{Player}` list plus a buffer with 13 bytes per entry) |
+| Drawing | Server parts, replicated to every client | Parts that exist only on each client. Your own ghost is drawn from your cursor every frame. Other ghosts glide toward their latest cell |
+| Late joiners | Replication | The server answers a client's first message with a snapshot of all visible ghosts |
+
+Placing blocks (`PlaceBlock`, `BlockPlacer`, `BlockPlacement.server`) is unchanged.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+Approximate size of each piece of a message:
+
+| Piece | Bytes |
+|---|---|
+| RemoteEvent call overhead (remote reference, argument count) plus reliable-message header | ~12 |
+| Short string such as `"Brick"` (type tag, length, characters) | ~7 |
+| `Vector3` (type tag + 3 × float32) | 13 |
+| `number` (type tag + float64) | 9 |
+| `Player` reference in a table | ~6 |
+| `buffer` header | ~3, plus its contents |
+| Replicated property change on an anchored part (instance reference, property id, axis-aligned CFrame) | ~25 |
+
+I left out UDP/IP and packet headers (~30 bytes per packet). Roblox batches many messages into each
+packet, so they're a small, shared cost in both designs.
+
+Scenario: **12 players, all building**, with frame rates split evenly: 4 at 60 FPS, 4 at 144,
+and 4 at 240. While someone builds, their cursor crosses about **8 grid cells per second**
+(cells are 4 studs). That cell rate decides the traffic in the new design.
+
+## Before
+
+**One client's upload.** Each `MoveGhost` is about 12 + 7 + 13 = **32 B**, sent once per frame:
+
+| FPS | Messages/s | Upload |
+|---|---|---|
+| 60 | 60 | 1.9 KB/s |
+| 144 | 144 | 4.6 KB/s |
+| 240 | 240 | 7.7 KB/s |
+
+This happens whether or not the cursor moves. With placing off, the client still sends
+`(nil, nil)` (~14 B) every frame.
+
+**Server inbound.** 4 × (60 + 144 + 240) = **1,776 messages/s**. At 32 B each that's about
+**57 KB/s**. Every one is a reliable message that the server acknowledges, runs a Lua handler for,
+and uses to write to a part's properties.
+
+**Server outbound.** Writing a property with the value it already has doesn't replicate, so in
+practice only cell changes go out: 12 builders × 8 changes/s × 11 other clients × 25 B ≈
+**26 KB/s total, about 2.2 KB/s per client**. There are about 88 property updates per second per
+client, and the builder also gets their own ghost back.
+
+**Why ping climbed.** The inbound messages make up a reliable, ordered stream that grows with
+frame rate rather than with what the player is doing. A few players at high FPS fill the queue.
+Each message waits behind the ones before it, so everything on the connection slows down, and that
+is the 300–400 ms ping. The ghost updates queued in bursts behind that backlog and were then
+replicated with no smoothing, which is why ghosts jumped around.
+
+## After
+
+**One client's upload.** Each `GhostUpdate` is about 12 + 9 + 13 = **34 B**, sent only on change
+and no more than 20 per second:
+
+| Case | Messages/s | Upload |
+|---|---|---|
+| Cursor still, or placing off | 0 | 0 |
+| Building, 8 cells/s | 8 | 0.27 KB/s |
+| Worst case (sweeping across more than 20 cells/s) | 20 | 0.68 KB/s |
+
+Frame rate no longer matters.
+
+**Server inbound.** 12 × 8 = **96 messages/s, about 3.3 KB/s**. The worst case is 240
+messages/s, about 8.2 KB/s.
+
+**Server outbound.** At most 20 broadcasts a second, and none on ticks where nothing changed. A
+broadcast with *k* entries is about 12 (call) + 3 + 6k (player list) + 3 + 13k (buffer) ≈
+**18 + 19k B**.
+
+- Typical: 96 changes/s spread over 20 ticks gives k ≈ 4.8, so ≈ 109 B × 20 ≈ **2.2 KB/s per
+  client**, and × 12 clients ≈ **26 KB/s total**.
+- Worst case, with all 12 changing on every tick: 18 + 19 × 12 = 246 B × 20 ≈ **4.9 KB/s per
+  client, 59 KB/s total**. The old design reaches about the same level when everyone sweeps
+  20 cells/s: 12 × 20 × 11 × 25 B ≈ 66 KB/s.
+
+## Summary (12 players building)
+
+| | Before | After | Change |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Upload per client (144 FPS) | 4.6 KB/s, 144 msg/s | 0.27 KB/s, ≤ 20 msg/s | ~17× less |
+| Upload per client (240 FPS) | 7.7 KB/s, 240 msg/s | 0.27 KB/s, ≤ 20 msg/s | ~28× less |
+| Upload while idle | 1.9–7.7 KB/s | 0 | gone |
+| Server inbound | ~1,776 msg/s, ~57 KB/s | ~96 msg/s, ~3.3 KB/s | ~18× less |
+| Server outbound | ~26 KB/s, ~88 updates/s per client | ~26 KB/s, ≤ 20 messages/s per client | about the same bytes, fewer messages |
+| Server total | ~83 KB/s | ~29 KB/s | ~3× less |
+
+Most of the saving is on the inbound side: the message flood that drove ping up is gone. Outbound
+bytes stay about the same because they were already capped by cell changes. They now go out as at
+most 20 batched messages per second, though, instead of a stream of separate property updates.
+
+**More players.** Upload per client stays fixed. Outbound per client is at most
+20 × (18 + 19N) B. At N = 30 that's about 11.8 KB/s, and only if every player moves a cell on every
+tick. Typical use is a fraction of that. If servers get much bigger, the next step would be to send
+each client only the ghosts near its camera.
+
+## What players will notice
+
+1. **Your own ghost is more responsive.** It's drawn locally on the same frame as your cursor.
+   Before, it was a server part, so it trailed your mouse by a full round trip, and by 300–400 ms
+   when the server was congested.
+2. **Other players' ghosts glide between cells instead of jumping.** Each one eases toward its
+   latest cell, closing about two thirds of the gap every 50 ms. Jumps over 8 cells (32 studs),
+   or a ghost reappearing after being hidden, snap straight to the new spot so a ghost doesn't
+   sweep across the map.
+3. **Other ghosts run a little behind.** On top of normal latency there's up to 50 ms waiting for
+   the next broadcast (25 ms on average) and roughly 50 ms of easing. The client's 50 ms send
+   limit only kicks in when the cursor moves faster than 20 cells a second, because the first
+   change after a pause goes out at once. That's about 75–125 ms behind an ideal, uncongested
+   connection. Before, under load, they were 300–400 ms behind and jumpy.
+4. **Fast sweeps look rounded.** If someone moves across more than 20 cells a second, the cells in
+   between aren't sent. Their ghost glides along a smoothed path instead of stepping through
+   every cell.
+5. **Ghosts are local now.** `Workspace.Ghosts` exists separately on each client. The server view
+   in Studio shows no ghosts. Nothing else in the project reads that folder.
